## Real Firefox Unicode-space Markdown source download (partial)

A live Firefox/BiDi localhost regression now edits the authenticated source
field to each of four Unicode-only spacing glyphs: NBSP, em space, thin space
and narrow NBSP. The actual download control enables and emits an exact UTF-8
Blob for each, without inventing a font or a fallback glyph. The same test
checks BOM-prefixed NBSP and refuses both BOM-only and ASCII-only spacing.

The regression compares raw Blob octets, not just decoded `Blob.text()`,
which drops the initial UTF-8 BOM on readback even when the file preserves it.
The same exact browser-captured BOM-prefixed Markdown bytes now reopen in
an independent native Rust CLI with an explicitly authored NBSP glyph. Real
PDF and transparent RGBA PNG output match the BOM-free inline Unicode source
byte-for-byte, and the PNG contains actual selected blue ink pixels. An
unsupported em-space glyph refuses before replacing the accepted PDF.

The browser download guard now checks the original UTF-8 byte budget
before determining whether the source is empty. Even structural-only source
larger than 4,096 bytes returns an explicit size refusal without scanning
unbounded pasted text, including BOM-prefixed text and Unicode separators.

This does not supply a saved semantic notebook or in-browser PDF compilation.

